// Kiddo School — Emotions & Feelings (Class 13 · age 2) · Learning Path lessons.
// One parent-led teaching lesson per flashcard. Written fresh for two-year-olds:
// every feeling is allowed, every behaviour has limits, faces come before words,
// and a point or a giggle counts as an answer. Every "notice" block references
// only what the card's alt text verifies ("2D illustrated child showing a happy
// feeling" and friends — emLesson in src/lessons.mjs); no artwork details beyond
// that are claimed. Library card pages in data-emotions-and-feelings.mjs stay a
// separate WHAT/WHY experience — no text is shared with them.
export const lpContent={
'happy':{
 title:'Feeling Happy',one:'The smile your toddler knows best, finally with a name — happy is the first feeling word and the easiest to catch in the act.',
 seoTitle:'Happy Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: name happy while it happens, make the face together in a mirror, and let giggles do most of the teaching.',
 intro:'This lesson starts the feelings deck where every toddler already lives: happy. The card gives the grin a face to study, and the day supplies a hundred real moments to name it.',
 what:'Happy is the feeling that arrives with a grin, a bounce and a shout — the one your toddler produces daily without any help. The card puts a face on it so the word has somewhere to stick.',
 mean:'Feelings are invisible until they get faces and words. Naming happy while it is happening builds the bridge between what a child feels inside and what everyone can see — the start of all self-knowledge.',
 know:'Any grin counts as an answer: a copied smile, a pointed finger, or happy shouted mid-giggle. Nothing is being tested — you are simply giving the good feeling its name, over and over, while it lasts.',
 why:'Happy happens many times a day, free of charge. A feeling word with that many live rehearsals sticks fast — and once happy is named, every other feeling has a template to fit.',
 dev:'Feeling vocabulary, face-reading, and self-noticing — your toddler connecting their own inside weather to a word another person can understand.',
 vocab:'happy, smile, face, grin, glad, laugh',
 teach:'1) Show the card and name the feeling twice: happy — this face is feeling happy. Let your voice smile along with the word.\n2) Make the face together: pull your biggest grin, hold it, wobble it. A mirror doubles the fun if one is close.\n3) Catch happy live: next giggle, say it on the spot — you look happy! The word lands hardest in the very second the feeling is here.\n4) Accept every version: a grin, a point at the card, or a delighted happy! are all complete answers at two.\n5) Keep naming it all week — happy at the swing, happy at the snack, happy at the reunion — so the word attaches to the feeling, not to the card.',
 say:'"Happy! Look at this face — what a big smile. Can you do that happy face? There it is! You look happy right now. Happy, happy — the best feeling to say."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having a happy feeling — one face, one feeling, nothing else competing. Ask your toddler to find the face, then make one just like it at their own face.',
 ask:'"Where is the happy face?" "Can you make that face?" "Who looks happy right now?" "Did you feel happy today?"',
 childSay:'Expect happy delivered with the matching grin — a word worn on the face. Some toddlers answer by pointing at themselves, which is the self-noticing skill arriving early; some just giggle, and the giggle is an answer.',
 real:'Happy moments arrive all day without scheduling: the swing, the dog, the biscuit. Say the word at each one and the card keeps teaching from the drawer without being fetched.',
 play:'Happy hunt: make happy faces at each other across the table until someone laughs — then name what just happened. The laughter proves the feeling, and the naming proves the word.',
 check:'Next time a big grin appears, ask how do you feel? A happy, a grin, or a bounce all pass. Then show the card at a flat moment — if your toddler still finds the happy face, the picture has become the dictionary.',
 tips:'Name the feeling while it is happening, not after: the live version sticks. Stay light — this card should never become a demand to perform happiness; it is a label for something already there.',
 safety:'No safety angle here — happy is the lowest-risk lesson in the deck. The only note: let the feeling be real, not commanded; toddlers cannot manufacture smiles on request, and nobody should have to.'},
'sad':{
 title:'Feeling Sad',one:'Everyone feels sad sometimes, toddlers hardest of all — naming the feeling gives it a handle, a word and a listener.',
 seoTitle:'Sad Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: meet the sad face calmly, name the feeling when small sorrows come, and let cuddles finish the lesson.',
 intro:'This lesson introduces the down feeling with the same calm voice as all the others: sad gets a face on a card, a word that fits in a small mouth, and a grown-up who stays.',
 what:'Sad is the feeling that brings droopy shoulders, a wobbly lip and sometimes tears — the one that asks for company. The card shows it safely, where it can be looked at without living it.',
 mean:'A named sadness is a smaller sadness: the word gives your toddler something to hold while the feeling passes. Being allowed to look at sad on a card teaches that this feeling is normal, welcome and survivable.',
 know:'Expect pointing, a quiet face, or a solemn word — sad is a soft answer, not a performance. If tears arrive during the lesson, the lesson has arrived in real life: comfort first, card later.',
 why:'Small sorrows fill a toddler’s week — the dropped biscuit, the finished song, the door that closed. Each one is a live chance to say the word gently and mean it.',
 dev:'Feeling vocabulary for the hard feelings, comfort-seeking, and the deep lesson that all feelings are allowed — sad included, always.',
 vocab:'sad, tears, cuddle, feelings, soft, better',
 teach:'1) Show the card with a quiet voice: sad — this face is feeling sad. Let the calm in your voice do the first teaching.\n2) Make a sad face together in the mirror: droopy mouth, soft eyes. Two seconds is plenty — then let the faces rest.\n3) Ask where the sad face is and let your toddler find it; a gentle point is a full answer.\n4) When a real small sorrow shows up, name it low and slow: that made you sad. Then open your arms — the cuddle is part of the lesson, not a break from it.\n5) Return to the card afterwards, once the tears have dried: you felt sad, and now you feel better. The full arc, told in eight words.',
 say:'"Sad. This little face is feeling sad — see the soft eyes? Sometimes we all feel sad. If you feel sad, come and find me. Cuddles help. Sad goes away, and love stays."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having a sad feeling — one quiet face to study. Ask your toddler to find the sad face, then to show you their own sad face, soft and slow.',
 ask:'"Where is the sad face?" "Can you do a sad face?" "What helps when you feel sad?" "Who gives good cuddles?"',
 childSay:'A solemn point, a practiced droopy face, or sad whispered like a secret — all are answers. Some toddlers pat the card kindly; the comforting instinct is the deepest version of this lesson.',
 real:'Real sad moments are the lesson: name the feeling when it comes, stay close, and let the tears run their course. The card can wait on the shelf — the feeling cannot.',
 play:'Cuddle corner: when sadness visits, take your toddler and the card to a soft spot together. Look at the sad face, say the word once, and let the cuddle do the rest of the talking.',
 check:'After a real sad moment has passed, show the card and ask what happened. A point, a sad face, or the word itself means the feeling and the label have met. Comfort given and received is the pass mark, every time.',
 tips:'Your calm is the whole atmosphere: sad named in a steady voice teaches that the feeling is survivable. Resist fixing fast — the sitting-with is the skill, and it is your skill as much as theirs.',
 safety:'Sad is allowed, always; tears are allowed too. The honest note: if sadness lingers for days, eats the appetite or the sleep, that is beyond a card — bring it to your health visitor or doctor, who are there for exactly this.'},
'angry':{
 title:'Feeling Angry',one:'The hottest feeling in the deck gets its name before the fireworks — angry is easier to survive with a word attached.',
 seoTitle:'Angry Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: meet the angry face in a calm moment, give the roar a word, and agree the rule — feelings yes, hurting no.',
 intro:'This lesson teaches angry on purpose, in a peaceful moment, long before the next storm: the face gets studied, the word gets practised, and the rule gets agreed while everyone is calm.',
 what:'Angry is the hot feeling — clenched fists, loud voice, a body that wants to explode. The card shows the face with no thunder attached, which is exactly why it works.',
 mean:'Anger named in advance becomes survivable in the moment: a toddler who owns the word has a handrail while the feeling roars. The feeling is allowed; what the body does with it has rules.',
 know:'Play the card when everyone is calm, not mid-meltdown. In the storm, keep it to two words — you are angry — and save the rest for after, when the card and the story can meet again.',
 why:'Anger visits every day at two: the block that fell, the shoe that lost, the biscuit that is gone. A word and a plan turn each storm from a catastrophe into weather.',
 dev:'Feeling vocabulary for big storms, body-awareness, and the first behaviour rule — all feelings are welcome, hurting is not.',
 vocab:'angry, cross, roar, stomp, storm, calm',
 teach:'1) Show the card in a calm moment and name it strongly: angry — this face is feeling angry. Let the word sound big and safe at once.\n2) Make the face together: scrunch, frown, growl a little roar. The pretending gives the feeling a rehearsal room with no consequences.\n3) Give the storm its safe outlets, agreed in advance: stomp your feet, squeeze your fists, roar at the ceiling — angry is allowed to be loud.\n4) Say the rule plainly, while everyone is smiling: all feelings are welcome, and hurting is not. We stomp, we squeeze, we never hit.\n5) In the next real storm, keep it tiny: you are angry. Two words, then quiet company. Afterwards, return to the card and let the story be told.',
 say:'"Angry! This face is feeling angry — look at those eyebrows. Can you make an angry face? Grrr! When you feel angry, stomp and roar — and I will stay right here. All feelings are welcome; hurting is not."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having an angry feeling — one stormy face, safely framed. Ask your toddler to find the angry face, then to show you their best pretend growl.',
 ask:'"Where is the angry face?" "Can you make that face?" "What can we do when we feel angry?" "Is hitting allowed?"',
 childSay:'A stomping march around the rug, a growl at the ceiling, or angry barked at the card itself — all are the word doing its work. Some toddlers go quiet and point; the pointing counts exactly the same.',
 real:'Storms arrive on their own schedule. In the moment, the word plus your calm presence is the whole lesson; the card comes back out afterwards, when the retelling turns a meltdown into a story with an ending.',
 play:'Stomp-and-roar corner: when the thunder starts, walk together to one safe spot and let the angry body do its permitted thing — stomping, roaring, squeezing a cushion. Then the storm always ends, and you are still there.',
 check:'After the next storm passes, show the card and ask what that feeling was. Angry, said or pointed, means the word is moving in. Feet that stomped and hands that stayed kind mean the rule landed too.',
 tips:'Keep yourself dull and steady through real storms — your steadiness is the teaching. Afterwards, retell it warmly: you were angry, you stomped, it passed. The story teaches more than the storm ever could.',
 safety:'The line every toddler needs spoken out loud: all feelings are welcome, hurting is never allowed. Storms get safe outlets — stomping, roaring, cushion-squeezing — and a grown-up who stays close without joining in.'},
'scared':{
 title:'Feeling Scared',one:'Wide eyes happen to everyone — the word scared makes the feeling sayable, and the grown-up nearby part of the plan.',
 seoTitle:'Scared Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: study the scared face safely, name the feeling when it visits, and make your calm presence the ending of every story.',
 intro:'This lesson lets your toddler study fear from the safest seat there is: a card, a cuddle, and a word. Nobody here has to be brave — the feeling just gets to have a name.',
 what:'Scared is the wide-eyed feeling that arrives with loud noises, big dogs and dark corners — the one that sends small bodies behind trusted legs. The card shows it without any danger attached.',
 mean:'A fear named is a fear that can be talked about: your toddler learns that scared is a feeling everyone has, and that saying it brings help instead of shame. Safety grows from company, not from pushing.',
 know:'Study the face, make it gently if your toddler wants to, and never demand bravery. If a real fright arrives, the order is fixed: feeling named, closeness given, story told afterwards.',
 why:'Frights are daily at two — the hoover, the thunder, the hand-dryer. A word that works mid-scare turns each one into a moment of connection instead of chaos.',
 dev:'Feeling vocabulary for the wobbliest feeling, comfort-seeking, and the trust loop — fear named, grown-up arrives, world made safe again.',
 vocab:'scared, hide, cuddle, safe, brave, here',
 teach:'1) Show the card softly: scared — this face is feeling scared. Wide eyes, tucked-in arms. Let your toddler look as long as they like.\n2) Make the face together, briefly and kindly: wide eyes, small squeak. Then let the faces rest — pretending is a game, not an exam.\n3) Say the ending every story needs: when you feel scared, I come to you, and we stay close until the scared feeling goes.\n4) If a real fright happens, name it in one breath — that noise gave you a scare — then scoop, settle, stay. The card can join in afterwards.\n5) Retell the fright gently once it is over: the noise happened, you felt scared, I came, and then it was fine. Stories with endings shrink fears.',
 say:'"Scared. This face is feeling scared — big wide eyes. Everyone feels scared sometimes. When you feel scared, say scared, and I will come. I stay close. The scared feeling gets smaller with company."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having a scared feeling — one wide-eyed face to study from a safe distance. Ask your toddler to find the scared face, and to point at what the arms are doing.',
 ask:'"Where is the scared face?" "Can you make a scared face?" "What helps when you feel scared?" "Who comes when you call?"',
 childSay:'A point from a safe lap, a whispered scared, or hiding the card behind a cushion — all are answers, and the hiding is honest too. Some toddlers simply lean into you; leaning is the lesson working.',
 real:'Real frights get the same script every time: name the feeling, offer the closeness, add the ending later. Your toddler learns the sequence by heart — feeling, word, grown-up, calm.',
 play:'Brave-and-safe game: hide a toy somewhere mild, find it together, and narrate — the toy felt scared, then you came, now it is happy. Your toddler gets to be the rescuer, which is the deepest way to learn the script.',
 check:'After the next fright, ask later: what did you feel? A scared, a point at the card, or hiding behind your leg with eye contact all pass. The real pass mark is the arm that reaches for you — trust installed.',
 tips:'Take fears at face value and answer them with company, not logic. Skip teasing entirely — a mocked fear teaches hiding, not courage. Your calm is the shelter; the word is the door into it.',
 safety:'All feelings are welcome, and scared most of all needs saying out loud. Never push a frightened child toward the scary thing — closeness first, retreat freely offered, and progress at the pace of the child.'},
'surprised':{
 title:'Feeling Surprised',one:'Eyebrows up, mouth open — surprise is the funniest feeling on the card and the easiest one to manufacture on purpose.',
 seoTitle:'Surprised Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: make the surprised face, play gentle hide-and-reveal games, and name the oh! the moment it happens.',
 intro:'This lesson turns the deck’s funniest face into a game: surprise, manufactured gently at home, named out loud the instant the eyebrows shoot up.',
 what:'Surprised is the sudden feeling — eyebrows high, mouth open, a gasp before the laugh. It arrives when the world does something unexpected, and toddlers meet it daily: peek-a-boo, pop-up toys, disappearing biscuits.',
 mean:'Surprise is the friendly edge of the big feelings: it startles without hurting, and it teaches that feelings arrive suddenly and pass quickly. It is also the gateway card — once the face game is fun, the heavier faces feel lighter.',
 know:'The gasp is the answer: a surprised oh!, a squeal, or both eyebrows climbing are complete. Manufacture the feeling small and kind — gentle reveals, never jumps — and name it while the eyebrows are still up.',
 why:'Surprise hides inside the games toddlers already play: covered cups, pop-up books, socks that turn out to be on hands. Every reveal is a rehearsal with the word attached.',
 dev:'Feeling vocabulary, sudden-change tolerance, and face-reading — plus the comedy timing that makes toddlers the best audiences on earth.',
 vocab:'surprised, wow, oh, peek, pop, gasp',
 teach:'1) Show the card with drama: surprised — look at those eyebrows! Let your own eyebrows join in from the first word.\n2) Make the face together: hands on cheeks, mouth open, a big ohh! Practise it twice, then keep it in your pockets for later.\n3) Play a gentle reveal: hide a toy under a tea towel, count to two, whisk it off and gasp surprised! together. Repeat until the giggles take over.\n4) Name real surprises live: the toast pops, the dog barks, the bubble pops — surprised! The word should chase the feeling by half a second.\n5) Let your toddler run the reveal: their turn to cover, count and lift, while you do the gasping. Being the surpriser teaches the timing from the inside.',
 say:'"Surprised! Oh — look at that face! Eyebrows up, mouth open: ohh! Watch the cup… one, two… pop! Surprised! Can you do the surprised face? Now you hide the toy and surprise me!"',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having a surprised feeling — one open-mouthed face, eyebrows high. Ask your toddler to find the surprised face, then to point at the mouth and the eyebrows that tell the story.',
 ask:'"Where is the surprised face?" "Can you make that face?" "What happened — pop! How do you feel?" "Whose turn to surprise?"',
 childSay:'A gasp, a squeal, or surprised delivered as a delighted brrrp! — the face will arrive long before the word does. Some toddlers stage the whole reveal themselves and gasp on your behalf; that is directing, and it counts.',
 real:'Surprises pepper the day without any planning: the kettle clicks, the light goes on, a friend appears. Catch two a day with the word and the feeling owns its name by Friday.',
 play:'Peek-cup theatre: three cups, one toy, slow reveals with big gasps. Let your toddler swap which cup hides the toy while your back is turned — being surprised well is a gift you give them.',
 check:'Next gentle surprise, watch the face: eyebrows up means the feeling arrived, and a surprised or an ohh means the word rode along. Then ask later where the surprised face is — a quick find closes the loop.',
 tips:'Keep every manufactured surprise kind and slow — this game lives next door to startle, and startle is not the lesson. If your toddler dislikes surprises, study the face on the card and skip the reveals; the word still lands.',
 safety:'Surprises stay small and voluntary: no jump-scares, no hiding out of sight, no loud bangs — a startled toddler is a frightened one, and the difference matters. Reveal, gasp, giggle, repeat.'},
'excited':{
 title:'Feeling Excited',one:'The wiggles have a name too — excited is the feeling before good things, and it is loud, bouncy and completely allowed.',
 seoTitle:'Excited Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: name the wiggles excited, let excited bodies bounce safely, and let the word do some of the settling.',
 intro:'This lesson names the feeling your toddler wears most afternoons: the pre-park wiggles, the pre-birthday bounce. Excited gets a face on a card and a permission slip for the noise.',
 what:'Excited is the can’t-sit-still feeling — bouncing, squealing, talking fast, wanting the good thing to start now. The card shows it as a face; your toddler shows it as weather.',
 mean:'Named excitement sometimes settles by itself: a body that hears you are so excited! often wobbles a little slower, because the feeling has been seen. Naming is not a cure-all — it is a hand on the shoulder.',
 know:'The feeling is allowed, loudly. The behaviour has banks to run between: bouncing yes, bouncing off the sofa no. Expect excited, a wiggle-demo, or a squeal as answers — all three pass.',
 why:'Excitement precedes most of the best moments of toddler life, which makes this the highest-frequency feeling in the deck. The word gets daily rehearsals for free.',
 dev:'Feeling vocabulary for high energy, body-awareness, and the first hint that naming a feeling helps steer it — emotional literacy in its native habitat.',
 vocab:'excited, wiggle, bounce, wow, ready, big',
 teach:'1) Show the card and name it with energy: excited — this face cannot sit still! Let your own body wiggle the word.\n2) Make the face and the wiggle together: wide eyes, bouncing shoulders, hands a-flutter. Two wiggly seconds, then settle.\n3) Catch the real thing live: before the park, before dinner guests, before anything good — you are excited! The word arrives while the wiggles are at their best.\n4) Give the feeling its safe channels: bounce on the spot, wiggle like jelly, squeeze-and-release. Excited bodies get to move — just not off furniture.\n5) After the good thing happens, retell it: you were so excited, and then — the park! The word and the memory file together.',
 say:'"Excited! Look at this face — so wiggly! Are you excited? Bounce like this — wiggle, wiggle, wiggle! Excited is a big, loud feeling and it is allowed. And when we get there… wow."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having an excited feeling — one wide-awake face full of energy. Ask your toddler to find the excited face, then to show you their own wiggle.',
 ask:'"Where is the excited face?" "Can you wiggle like that?" "Are you excited right now?" "What is coming that is good?"',
 childSay:'A bounce that says the word for them, a squealed excited!, or both hands waving — the body is the main speaker here. Some toddlers freeze mid-wiggle when named, surprised to be seen; the freeze is the word landing.',
 real:'Excitement announces itself daily: shoes by the door, the kettle for bath time, a friend at the window. Name it before the event, and the word becomes part of the anticipation — which is half the fun.',
 play:'Wiggle stop-dance: excited music on, everyone wiggles; music off, everyone freezes. The freeze is the skill — a body that can go from full bounce to still is a body learning its own volume dial.',
 check:'Before the next good thing, ask how do you feel? An excited, a bounce, or a wiggly nod all pass. Then watch for the word appearing unprompted in your toddler’s own mouth — that is the graduation.',
 tips:'Say the feeling instead of calm down, which has never once worked in the history of toddlers. Naming first, wiggle-channel second, expectation third: excitement managed is excitement enjoyed.',
 safety:'The feeling is always allowed; the body has banks to stay within — no bouncing on sofas, no sprinting toward roads, no wiggling at the top of the stairs. Give the energy a floor, and it gives you a show.'},
'tired':{
 title:'Feeling Tired',one:'The droopy feeling at the end of a big day — tired, named out loud, can turn a storm into a nap before it starts.',
 seoTitle:'Tired Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: meet the tired face, name the feeling before the meltdowns, and let the word smooth the road to rest.',
 intro:'This lesson teaches the feeling that arrives every evening whether anyone invites it or not: tired. The card gives the droop a face, and the wind-down gives it a routine.',
 what:'Tired is the end-of-day feeling — heavy eyelids, slow body, short temper. Toddlers show it constantly and fight it constantly, which is exactly why the word is worth its weight in calm.',
 mean:'A named tired feeling can be surrendered to: your body is tired, it is asking for rest works on a toddler in a way bedtime does not. The word turns rest from a punishment into an answer.',
 know:'Expect a yawn, a droopy demonstration, or a tired whispered from under a fringe. Name the feeling early — before the storm — and let the routine carry the rest.',
 why:'Tired visits daily, usually on schedule, and it sits behind a fair share of afternoon meltdowns. A word for it is a fire extinguisher mounted by the door.',
 dev:'Feeling vocabulary for body signals, self-noticing, and the habit of reading your own body — the earliest form of self-care.',
 vocab:'tired, sleepy, rest, yawn, bed, quiet',
 teach:'1) Show the card at a quiet moment: tired — this face is feeling tired. Let your voice drop low, because tired words travel best softly.\n2) Make the face together: droopy eyes, a big theatrical yawn, shoulders sliding down. The pretending is gentle comedy and it lands.\n3) Name the real thing early in the evening: your body feels tired — look, the tired face! Say it before the tears, not after.\n4) Connect the word to the routine: tired means rest — pyjamas, book, bed. The feeling has a plan, and the plan is kind.\n5) In the morning, close the loop: you were tired, you slept, and now look at you. The arc teaches that tired always has an answer.',
 say:'"Tired. This face is feeling tired — sleepy eyes, big yawn. Is your body tired? Tired means time for rest. Come here, let us be quiet together. Sleep is coming, and that is good."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having a tired feeling — one droopy, sleepy face. Ask your toddler to find the tired face, then to make their very best yawn next to it.',
 ask:'"Where is the tired face?" "Can you yawn like that?" "Is your body tired?" "What does tired mean it is time for?"',
 childSay:'A yawn caught and copied, a droopy slide to the floor, or tired murmured from inside a cuddle — all are answers. Some toddlers point at the card and then at themselves; that pointing is self-knowledge arriving.',
 real:'Tired shows up on time every evening and mid-morning after big outings. Name it at the first yawn and the word becomes the beginning of the wind-down instead of the middle of a fight.',
 play:'Big-yawn theatre: take turns doing the hugest, silliest yawns together, then let the silliness deflate into quiet. The game ends the way the feeling ends — soft, slow, and ready for blankets.',
 check:'At the first evening yawn, ask how does your body feel? A tired, a yawn, or a lean into your shoulder all pass. A toddler who says tired and walks toward the cot has taken the whole lesson home.',
 tips:'Name tired before the wobbles start — the word works best at the first yawn, worst at the third meltdown. Pair it with the same routine every night; the feeling and the ritual learn each other.',
 safety:'Tired toddlers fall off things: the climbing urge and the droopy body are a bad mix, so low-energy play only after the word is said. Car-seat battles and tired bodies need extra patience and all the straps, every time.'},
'calm':{
 title:'Feeling Calm',one:'The quiet feeling worth practising on purpose — calm is the one your toddler will need to find again after every storm.',
 seoTitle:'Calm Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: practise slow breaths with the calm face, play quiet games, and build the feeling before anyone needs it.',
 intro:'This lesson practises the deck’s quietest face while nobody needs it: calm, rehearsed in soft moments so it can be found again in loud ones. Two breaths at a time — that is the whole method.',
 what:'Calm is the settled feeling — slow breath, easy face, a body that is not in a hurry. The card shows it as a face; your toddler can feel it in two slow breaths, right on cue.',
 mean:'Calm is the first feeling toddlers can switch on deliberately: breath is the one dial a two-year-old can actually turn. Practised daily, the slow breath becomes a tool the body owns before the words do.',
 know:'Expect a copy-cat breath, a soft sigh, or a solemn point — all are answers. Teach calm when everyone is already calm; asking for it mid-storm is like fishing in an empty bath.',
 why:'Every storm needs a harbour. The calm face, practised in peace, becomes the picture your toddler’s body remembers when the next big feeling blows through.',
 dev:'Breath control, self-regulation in embryo, and quiet-time habits — the skill that turns every later meltdown shorter and every bedtime softer.',
 vocab:'calm, breath, slow, quiet, soft, still',
 teach:'1) Show the card in a soft voice: calm — this face is feeling calm. Let the word itself arrive slowly.\n2) Breathe together: sniff an imaginary flower — breathe in slow — and waft a feather — breathe out long. Two rounds is plenty; five gets silly.\n3) Make the calm face in a mirror: loose shoulders, easy mouth, sleepy-blinking eyes. Let your toddler study their own quiet face.\n4) Play a quiet game — rocking a toy to sleep, listening for the fridge hum — and name the feeling during it: this is calm. We are being calm right now.\n5) Practise at odd, peaceful moments all week: after the bath, in the buggy, before the story. The rehearsed feeling becomes findable on demand.',
 say:'"Calm. This face is feeling calm — see the soft eyes? Let us breathe like the calm face: smell the flower… and blow the feather. Quiet body, slow breath. This is calm. Lovely."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having a calm feeling — one settled, easy face. Ask your toddler to find the calm face, then to make their own face soft and slow beside it.',
 ask:'"Where is the calm face?" "Can you make your face calm?" "Can you smell the flower with me?" "What do we do with a slow breath?"',
 childSay:'A copy-cat breath with a wobbly exhale, a sigh from the belly, or a body that suddenly settles — the body speaks first here. Some toddlers shush you gently; being calm together is the whole exercise.',
 real:'Calm moments hide in the day: the buggy ride, the bath, the last story. Say the word during two of them and the feeling gets a daily home — ready for the day it is needed as a rescue.',
 play:'Flower-and-feather: sniff an imaginary bloom (slow in), waft a feather off your palm (slow out). Do it side by side, then let your toddler lead. Two breaths, three times a day, builds a regulator.',
 check:'At the next small wobble, offer the breath: shall we smell the flower? A breath attempt, a settling body, or a point at the calm card all pass. Bodies that find calm faster than last week are the real report card.',
 tips:'Rehearse in peace or not at all — calm demanded mid-meltdown collapses. Keep your own breath slow and visible while you teach; toddlers borrow the nervous systems of the people they love.',
 safety:'Breathing games are the safest card in the deck; the honest note is about expectations — calm is a practice, not a switch, and storms still happen. Never use it as a punishment or a command to feel differently.'},
'shy':{
 title:'Feeling Shy',one:'Watching from behind a leg is a whole feeling with a name — shy — and taking a minute is completely allowed.',
 seoTitle:'Shy Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: name the shy feeling, let warm-up minutes be normal, and take the pressure off hello forever.',
 intro:'This lesson gives a word to the feeling every toddler knows from birthday parties: shy. The card makes it studyable, and the plan makes it normal — new people take a minute.',
 what:'Shy is the hold-back feeling — watching from a distance, quiet voice, wanting to join from behind a trusted knee. The card shows it gently, as a feeling that visits everyone sometimes.',
 mean:'Shyness named loses its sting: your toddler learns that taking a minute is a way of joining, not a failure to join. Warm-up time is how confidence is actually built — slowly, on the child’s own clock.',
 know:'Expect a point, a whisper, or a practiced hiding face — all answers. The lesson is permission: hello can wait, watching counts as taking part, and the minute belongs to the child.',
 why:'New people appear weekly — the plumber, the new cousin, the other toddler at the gate. A word and a plan for the pause turns each arrival from a test into a process.',
 dev:'Feeling vocabulary for the quiet feelings, social warm-up, and self-advocacy in seed form — the right to take your time, learned early.',
 vocab:'shy, quiet, watch, hello, minute, warm',
 teach:'1) Show the card quietly: shy — this face is feeling a bit shy. Keep your voice close and small, like the feeling itself.\n2) Make the face together: peek out from behind your hands. The hiding can be a game — peek-a-shy — with a smile waiting at the end.\n3) Say the plan out loud: when new people come, you can say hello, or wave, or watch from my knee. All of it counts.\n4) At the next real arrival, name it softly if needed: feeling a bit shy? That is okay. Then stand nearby and do nothing else — the minute is doing the work.\n5) Afterwards, close gently: you watched for a while, and then you waved. The story teaches that warm-ups have happy endings.',
 say:'"Shy. This face is feeling shy — peeking out, being quiet. It is okay to feel shy. New people take a minute. You can watch, you can wave, or you can say hello — whenever you are ready. I am right here."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having a shy feeling — one quiet, peeking face. Ask your toddler to find the shy face, then to peek out from behind their own hands just like it.',
 ask:'"Where is the shy face?" "Can you peek out like that?" "What can we do when we feel shy?" "Are you ready to wave yet?"',
 childSay:'A face half-hidden behind your leg, a whispered shy, or a slow emerging smile — all are the feeling speaking. A first tiny wave after minutes of watching is the milestone; nobody claps louder than they want.',
 real:'Every new face is a rehearsal: name the feeling only if your toddler wants it named, stand close, and let the warm-up run. The world becomes a place where shy has a script and an ending.',
 play:'Peek-a-shy at home: hide behind a door, a curtain, a teddy — and peek out with a slow smile. The game teaches that hiding and joining are neighbours, and the door between them opens from the child’s side.',
 check:'After the next warm-up, ask how you felt at first. A shy, a point at the card, or a hiding grin all pass. Then watch what came after — a wave, a hello, a joined game — and name that part too.',
 tips:'Take the performance pressure off entirely: hello is an offer, not a duty. Skip apologies to strangers for a shy toddler; a calm this is how they take a minute respects the child and ends the moment.',
 safety:'All feelings welcome — including the quiet ones, which need it most. A shy child is never pushed forward to perform: no forced hellos, no handed-over-to-strangers, no making shyness into a show for grown-ups.'},
'silly':{
 title:'Feeling Silly',one:'The tongue-out card your toddler will request daily — silly is play turned into a feeling, and the feeling is catching.',
 seoTitle:'Silly Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: pull silly faces together, name the giggles silly, and give the feeling its own time and place.',
 intro:'This lesson names the feeling behind the fart jokes and the walking-backwards: silly. The card gives the giggles a face, and the day gets a sanctioned time and place for pure nonsense.',
 what:'Silly is the playful feeling — tongue out, googly eyes, a body that has decided everything is funny. The card shows it as a face; your toddler has been demonstrating it for months.',
 mean:'Naming silly hands your toddler a word for deliberate play — the feeling they produce on purpose to make things fun. A feeling with a name can be requested, scheduled and shared, which is comedy in embryo.',
 know:'Expect an instant face, a giggle, or silly! shouted at full volume. The word lands fast because the feeling needs no introduction — your only job is the name and the venue.',
 why:'Silly is the deck’s release valve: ten seconds of deliberate nonsense can reset an afternoon. A named, scheduled silly beats a chaotic one every time.',
 dev:'Humour development, deliberate play, and emotional vocabulary for joy — plus the social skill of reading when silliness is welcome.',
 vocab:'silly, funny, face, giggle, goofy, joke',
 teach:'1) Show the card and meet it with a grin: silly — this face is being so silly. Let your eyebrows start the game.\n2) Pull silly faces together: tongue out, puffed cheeks, crossed eyes. A mirror makes it a tournament; keep the rounds short.\n3) Name the feeling while the giggles run: you are being so silly! The word attaches itself — no drilling required.\n4) Give silly its frame: silly time lives in play, not at the dinner table or the shop checkout. Say the frame once, kindly, and hold it.\n5) Let your toddler run a round: they make the face, you do the laughing. The comedian-audience swap is the social half of the lesson.',
 say:'"Silly! Look at this face — what a silly face! Can you make one? Tongue out… googly eyes… oh no, TOO silly! Silly is for playtime. Silly makes us laugh — your turn to be silly now."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having a silly feeling — one tongue-out, playful face. Ask your toddler to find the silly face, then to make their silliest face right back at it.',
 ask:'"Where is the silly face?" "Can you do a silly face?" "Who is being silly right now?" "When is silly time?"',
 childSay:'An instant tongue, a cross-eyed stare, or silly! barked like a command — the feeling arrives faster than any other card in the deck. Expect to be commanded to look; the audience is part of the game.',
 real:'Silly breaks out everywhere: the bath, the buggy, the wrong-hat game. Name it when it comes, enjoy it, and let the frame do the steering — silly named is silly shared.',
 play:'Silly-face tournament: two players, one mirror, faces escalate until someone laughs. Everyone loses, everyone wins — then a deep breath ends the tournament, and the silliness folds back into the afternoon.',
 check:'Ask at a calm moment: show me the silly face. An instant tongue-out is a pass with honours. Then watch the frame: a toddler who saves the silliest material for playtime has learned the whole lesson, frame included.',
 tips:'Join in fully — silly is the one feeling that dies without an audience. Keep the frame firm and cheerful: silly has a time, and grown-ups will say when it is. That boundary is what makes the game last.',
 safety:'Silly bodies bump into things: the tournament stays on soft floors, away from furniture corners and stairs. The honest note — silliness can tip into wildness fast, and the grown-up owns the timer, not the toddler.'},
'proud':{
 title:'Feeling Proud',one:'The chin-up feeling after the tower stands or the zip gives in — proud is a big-kid word that toddlers earn honestly.',
 seoTitle:'Proud Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: name the proud feeling right after a hard-won try, praise the effort, and let the chest-out glow last longer.',
 intro:'This lesson catches the feeling that arrives just after a hard thing works: proud. The card gives the chin-up moment a face, and your words give it a name that makes the glow last.',
 what:'Proud is the earned feeling — chest out, chin up, a smile that cannot sit still, arriving after something took real trying. The card shows it as a face; your toddler supplies the achievements.',
 mean:'Proud connects effort to feeling: your toddler learns that the warm glow after trying hard has a name, and that the name comes from inside them, not from a prize. That is the seed of healthy motivation.',
 know:'Name it at the moment of success and again at retelling: you did it — you look proud! Expect a grin, a cheer, or proud said with the chest puffed. All pass; the grin most of all.',
 why:'Toddlers achieve hard things hourly — stairs, zips, towers. Each achievement is a live chance to wire effort to feeling, and the wiring is free.',
 dev:'Achievement vocabulary, effort-praise habits, and self-esteem built on doing — the feeling that makes toddlers try again tomorrow.',
 vocab:'proud, did, try, again, wow, hard',
 teach:'1) Show the card and name the feeling: proud — this face did something hard and it worked! Let your chin lift as you say it.\n2) Make the face together: chest out, chin up, hands on hips. Superhero posture is the honest body language of this feeling.\n3) Catch it live: next tower, next zip, next step climbed — you did it! You look proud! The word lands while the glow is warm.\n4) Praise the trying out loud: you kept going, you tried again, you did it yourself. The effort is the story, not just the win.\n5) Retell it at bedtime: today you were proud, because you built the tower all by yourself. Named achievements become part of who your toddler is.',
 say:'"Proud! This face did something hard — look how it stands up tall! Can you stand like that? You did it! You kept trying and it worked. You must feel so proud. I am proud of you too."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having a proud feeling — one tall-standing, chin-up face. Ask your toddler to find the proud face, then to stand tall like it, hands on hips.',
 ask:'"Where is the proud face?" "Can you stand tall like that?" "What did you do today?" "How did it feel when it worked?"',
 childSay:'A chest-out march, a both-arms-up did it!, or proud delivered as poud with full posture — the body carries the word. Some toddlers point at the card and then at their own tower; that pointing is the connection.',
 real:'Achievements fill the day: the shoe that went on, the cup that got carried, the step that got jumped. One named proud per day keeps the word warm and the trying coming.',
 play:'Did-it parade: at the end of the day, march through three things your toddler did themselves, each with a proud-face salute. The parade teaches the feeling by collecting the evidence.',
 check:'After the next hard-won success, ask how do you feel? A proud, a chest-out grin, or a did it! all pass. Then listen for the word arriving unprompted in your toddler’s own retelling — that is the word moving in.',
 tips:'Praise the effort rather than the talent: words about keeping on build persistence, while clever-child talk quietly builds a fear of failing. Keep the word for genuine effort — your toddler’s own measure of hard is the one that counts.',
 safety:'No safety angle beyond cheering safely — the proud parade stays off the furniture. The honest note: let the child do the doing; help offered too fast steals the feeling this card exists to name.'},
'worried':{
 title:'Feeling Worried',one:'The furrowed brow for the small might-happens — worried feelings shrink when they get a word and a simple plan.',
 seoTitle:'Worried Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: name the worried feeling, preview what happens next, and let a simple plan do the comforting.',
 intro:'This lesson names the quiet, frowny feeling that comes before: a large dog, the strange cot, the first day somewhere new. Worried gets a word, and the word gets a plan.',
 what:'Worried is the before-feeling — furrowed brow, clingy body, questions in the eyes. It arrives when something might happen, and at two, most might-happens loom large.',
 mean:'Worries shrink when they meet a plan: first shoes, then hello, then home. A plain map of what comes next gives the feeling edges — and your calm does the rest of the reassuring.',
 know:'Expect a point, a frown-copy, or worried said small. Do not argue the worry away or promise outcomes; acknowledge it, sketch the plan, and stand close. Company is the cure that works.',
 why:'New things arrive weekly at two, and each one casts a shadow before it arrives. A named feeling with a named plan turns dread into a map your toddler can read.',
 dev:'Feeling vocabulary for anticipation, plan-making, and the trust loop — worry spoken, grown-up steady, world predictable again.',
 vocab:'worried, hmm, plan, next, together, here',
 teach:'1) Show the card gently: worried — this face is thinking about something that might happen. Furrowed brow and all.\n2) Make the face together, softly: small frown, chewing lip. Then let the face rest — the pretending stays light.\n3) Teach the tool: when you feel worried, we say worried, and then we make a plan. Plans make big feelings smaller.\n4) Rehearse on a small real one: the big dog tomorrow — first we look from the window, then we hold hands, then we decide. Say the plan in three beats.\n5) When a real worry visits, use the sequence: name it, plan it, stay close. Afterwards, tell how it went — the ending is the medicine.',
 say:'"Worried. This face is feeling worried — see the little frown? Everyone feels worried sometimes. When you do, tell me, and we will make a plan together. First this, then that — and I stay with you."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having a worried feeling — one furrowed, thinking face. Ask your toddler to find the worried face, then to make their own small thinking-frown beside it.',
 ask:'"Where is the worried face?" "Can you make a worried face?" "What helps when you feel worried?" "What is our plan?"',
 childSay:'A point at the card, a worried whispered into your shoulder, or a frown offered in miniature — all are answers. A toddler who brings the card during a wobbly moment has learned to ask for help with it.',
 real:'Worries announce themselves in clingy days and stalling bedtimes. Name the feeling, sketch the next three steps out loud, and let your steadiness answer the part words cannot.',
 play:'Plan-the-day game: line up three toys and give the day a map — first blocks, then snack, then park. Toddlers who can see the shape of what happens next carry fewer unnamed worries.',
 check:'After the next mapped event, ask how you felt before. A worried, a nod, or a point at the card all pass. The deeper pass mark: your toddler brings you the worry earlier next time — trust compounding.',
 tips:'Acknowledge before you reassure — yes, that dog is big comes first; the plan comes second. Skip promises about what will happen; your calm and the map together are the honest comfort.',
 safety:'All feelings welcome — including the pre-feelings that arrive early. Do not dismiss or mock a worry; acknowledge it and stay near. If worries grow big enough to run the day, a chat with your health visitor is the strong move, not a failure.'},
'frustrated':{
 title:'Feeling Frustrated',one:'Gritted teeth over a fallen tower — frustration is the daily weather of being two, and it deserves its own respectable word.',
 seoTitle:'Frustrated Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: name the frustrated feeling, offer help-or-try-again, and let the word carry some of the struggle.',
 intro:'This lesson names the feeling behind the block tower that fell again and the zip that will not budge: frustrated. The card hands the everyday tussle a face and a respectable name.',
 what:'Frustrated is the stuck feeling — gritted teeth, hot face, hands that want to throw. It visits when trying hard is not yet working, which at two is approximately hourly.',
 mean:'The word works like a small reservoir of patience: naming the feeling gives the storm a shape, and a shaped feeling is easier to sit inside. Frustration survived is where persistence is actually built.',
 know:'Offer two exits when it strikes: asking for help, or one more go. Either exit begins with the feeling named — that is frustrating — and the throwing of toys gets its gentle limit.',
 why:'Frustration is constant at two: cups that spill, lids that stick, towers that fall. A word this busy pays for itself before the week is out.',
 dev:'Persistence, feeling vocabulary for the struggle-zone, and choice-making — plus the deep lesson that hard feelings have words and exits.',
 vocab:'frustrated, hard, help, try, stuck, again',
 teach:'1) Show the card and name it clearly: frustrated — this face is trying and trying and it will not work! Gritted teeth and all.\n2) Make the face together: clenched jaw, squeezed fists, a small grrr. The pretending gives the feeling a dress rehearsal.\n3) Teach the two doors: when you feel frustrated, you can say help please — or try again. Both doors are always open.\n4) At the next real struggle, lead with the word — so frustrating! The tower fell again. Then offer the two doors and wait one beat longer than feels comfortable.\n5) Afterward, retell the survival: it was frustrating, you tried again, and look — it worked. Or: I helped, and together we did it. Both endings count.',
 say:'"Frustrated! This face is trying so hard — and it still will not work. Grrr! That is frustrating. You can say help, or you can try again. I am here either way. Hard things get easier with trying."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having a frustrated feeling — one gritted, working-hard face. Ask your toddler to find the frustrated face, then to make their own grrr face, fists and all.',
 ask:'"Where is the frustrated face?" "Can you make a grrr face?" "What are our two choices when we feel frustrated?" "Do you want help or try again?"',
 childSay:'A grrr with real feeling, help said as hep, or a determined second attempt with no words at all — all are the lesson working. A thrown cup means the feeling outran the word; name it, reset gently, keep the doors open.',
 real:'Struggles arrive all day: socks, lids, zips, puzzles. Name the feeling at the first grrr, offer the doors, and let the surviving do the teaching — frustration endured with company is persistence under construction.',
 play:'Stuck-point play: give your toddler a puzzle one step past easy, and stay close without fixing. When the grrr comes, offer the two doors and watch which one gets picked — then pick the other one yourself next time.',
 check:'At the next struggle, listen for the word: frustrated, hep, or a look at the card mid-grrr all pass. Then watch the second attempt — a toddler who tries once more is building the muscle this card exists for.',
 tips:'Put the word in ahead of the solutions — the vocabulary is the bucket, the doors come second. Hold back one beat longer than instinct suggests; the gap between stuck and helped is where persistence grows.',
 safety:'All feelings welcome, and frustration is the most frequent guest. The limit stands beside it: throwing and hitting are not on the menu — stomping, squeezing and the two doors are. Move breakables; keep the toddler.'},
'confused':{
 title:'Feeling Confused',one:'The puzzled face when the world changes its rules — confused is learning in progress, wearing a small frown.',
 seoTitle:'Confused Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: name the confused feeling, slow the moment down together, and turn what-on-earth into let-us-look.',
 intro:'This lesson names the face your toddler makes when routines bend: confused. The card gives the puzzlement a word, and slowing down gives it a way out.',
 what:'Confused is the what-is-happening feeling — tilted head, searching eyes, a pause mid-action. It arrives when the usual stops being usual: a different cup, a changed plan, a rule with edges nobody explained.',
 mean:'Confusion is what learning looks like from the inside: the mind holds two versions of the world and hunts for the edit. Named calmly, it becomes interesting instead of alarming — what instead of wail.',
 know:'Expect a head-tilt copy, a puzzled hmm, or a point between two options — all answers. When it happens live, drop to slow motion and give the moment words; the fog clears at walking pace, never at a run.',
 why:'The world changes its mind daily at two — the shop is shut, the cup is a new cup, Daddy wears a hat. A word for the in-between feeling keeps those moments curious instead of frightening.',
 dev:'Feeling vocabulary for the puzzlement-zone, flexibility when routines bend, and thinking out loud with a grown-up — cognitive skills with an emotional handle.',
 vocab:'confused, hmm, why, look, together, slow',
 teach:'1) Show the card and name it with a raised eyebrow: confused — this face is thinking, hmm, what is happening here?\n2) Make the face together: tilted head, small frown, a questioning hmm. The copy is easy and funny — let the mirror judge.\n3) Teach the exit: when you feel confused, we slow down and look together. Say the sequence twice so it settles.\n4) When a real puzzler arrives — the cup is different, the plan has changed — narrate slowly: hmm, this is confusing! Come and look with me. Then actually look, at toddler pace.\n5) Close every puzzling moment with the resolution: now we know! The arc from confused to understood is the lesson, repeated in miniature all week.',
 say:'"Confused. This face is thinking — hmm? What is happening? Everyone feels confused sometimes. When you do, we slow down and look together. Hmm… now we know! Confused always has an ending."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having a confused feeling — one tilted, puzzled face. Ask your toddler to find the confused face, then to make their own wondering hmm-face beside it.',
 ask:'"Where is the confused face?" "Can you make a hmm face?" "What do we do when we feel confused?" "What happened here?"',
 childSay:'A tilted head, an honest hmm, or a finger pointing between two things — all are the feeling on display. Some toddlers hand you the confusing object directly; delegation is a perfectly good strategy.',
 real:'Puzzling moments appear without warning: the new cup, the closed park, the haircut. Name the feeling, slow the moment, look together — and the day’s surprises become problems the two of you solve.',
 play:'Mystery object: pop a household thing in a sock and let your toddler feel, frown and guess. The confused face is the point of the game — celebrate the hmm as loudly as the answer.',
 check:'At the next puzzling change, watch for the tilt or listen for the hmm — either passes. Then ask later where the confused face is: a quick find plus a remembered hmm means the word and the feeling have met.',
 tips:'Slow is the whole trick — narrate what is happening in short steps and let your toddler watch you figure it out too. Confusion shared and solved teaches that not-knowing is a stop on the way, not a wall.',
 safety:'All feelings welcome — confusion included, no matter how small the trigger looks from up here. The honest note: confusion during big changes (moves, new siblings, new beds) deserves extra slowness and extra cuddles, not just a word.'},
'bored':{
 title:'Feeling Bored',one:'The flat, floppy, nothing-is-fun feeling — boredom is uncomfortable and strangely useful: it is where ideas are born.',
 seoTitle:'Bored Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: name the bored feeling, plant one small play idea, and let imagination take the invitation and run.',
 intro:'This lesson names the feeling that arrives on quiet afternoons: bored. The card gives the floppiness a face, and the lesson plants one small idea and stands back.',
 what:'Bored is the nothing-feels-interesting feeling — flopped body, distant stare, toys that were fine yesterday and are suddenly wrong. The card shows it honestly, as a feeling everyone gets.',
 mean:'Boredom is the doorway to invented play: a toddler given one tiny idea at the bored moment usually spins it into twenty minutes. The feeling is uncomfortable on purpose — it is the mind asking for a job.',
 know:'Expect a flop, a whine, or bored said like a complaint — all answers, and the complaint is the invitation. Name the feeling, offer one seed, not a schedule, and let the growing be theirs.',
 why:'Quiet hours arrive daily, and a toddler who owns the word for the lull can move through it faster. You are not the entertainment department — you are the seed merchant.',
 dev:'Imagination, self-directed play, and feeling vocabulary for the flat moods — the earliest independence training there is.',
 vocab:'bored, idea, play, new, toys, hmm',
 teach:'1) Show the card on a suitably floppy afternoon: bored — this face cannot find anything fun. Sympathise first; the feeling is real.\n2) Make the face together: droopy shoulders, hollow sigh, a bored ahh. The drama gets a laugh, which is already half a cure.\n3) Teach the move: when you feel bored, tell me — and we will find one new idea together. One idea, not ten.\n4) Plant the seed small: set two toys side by side, say one sentence — the dolly is hungry, I wonder — and walk a step away. The distance is deliberate.\n5) When the play catches, leave it alone. Come back later and name what grew: you were bored, and look what you invented! The arc is the lesson.',
 say:'"Bored. This face feels bored — nothing feels fun. Hmm! When you feel bored, tell me, and we will find one little idea. Look — two blocks… I wonder what they could be. Now it is your game."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having a bored feeling — one flat, floppy face. Ask your toddler to find the bored face, then to do their very best bored slouch beside it.',
 ask:'"Where is the bored face?" "Can you make a bored face?" "What could we do first?" "What did you invent?"',
 childSay:'A floppy slump, a whined bored, or a shrug with eyebrows — the feeling arrives in body language first. A toddler who answers the seed with their own twist has already left the lesson behind, which is the goal.',
 real:'Flat afternoons come every week. Name the feeling, plant one idea, resist the rescue — and the empty stretch slowly turns into the birthplace of the best games.',
 play:'Two-toy spark: choose any two toys that rarely meet, set them side by side, and wonder out loud for exactly one sentence. Then be busy elsewhere; the spark catches best unwatched.',
 check:'After the next floppy patch, ask how you felt before the game. Bored, a shrug, or a finger landing on the floppy face all pass. The longer pass mark: stretches of invented play growing quietly longer, week on week.',
 tips:'Name the feeling, plant the seed, then genuinely step back — boredom plus one idea is the recipe, boredom plus a show is the trap. You do not have to fill every flat minute; nobody could.',
 safety:'Bored toddlers go hunting, and their hunting grounds include stairs, cupboards and the bin: keep the floppy zones safe and doors latched. The honest note — a bored two-year-old left entirely alone finds trouble; nearby-but-busy is the sweet spot.'},
'hurt':{
 title:'Feeling Hurt',one:'Ouch is often the first feeling word a toddler says — hurt gets comfort first, and its name right after the tears settle.',
 seoTitle:'Hurt Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: meet the hurt face, build the comfort-first routine, and give the word its turn once the tears are done.',
 intro:'This lesson handles the feeling every toddler meets at ground level: hurt. The card gives the ouch a face, and the routine gives it an order — cuddle first, word second.',
 what:'Hurt is the ouch feeling — a stumble, a bonk, a pinch, arriving with tears before anyone can explain. The card shows a mild hurt face, safely framed, for studying after the fact.',
 mean:'Comfort comes first because bodies shout louder than minds: a hurt child needs arms before vocabulary. Once the tears settle, the word hurt gives the memory a shape — and retold stumbles stop running the whole afternoon.',
 know:'Expect ouch delivered at volume, tears, or a point at the sore bit. The lesson is the order: comfort, settle, then name — this card joins in afterwards, never in the first seconds.',
 why:'Stumbles are daily and mostly small. A standing routine — cuddle, calm, name, retell — turns each bonk from a drama into an event with a beginning and an end.',
 dev:'Feeling vocabulary for body signals, comfort-seeking and comfort-giving, and memory-shaping — small stories that make sense of small injuries.',
 vocab:'hurt, ouch, bump, cuddle, kiss, better',
 teach:'1) Show the card at a calm moment: hurt — this face has an ouch. See the tears? They are already drying. Keep it light.\n2) Practise the routine backwards, in play: kiss a teddy’s sore paw, wrap it in a cuddle, and say the words — hurt, cuddle, better. Your toddler will run this script for real.\n3) Say the order out loud: when you feel hurt, cuddles come first, and the word comes after. Both are coming; the arms are just faster.\n4) At the next real bonk: scoop, settle, comfort — no lesson yet. Once the sobs slow, name it softly: that hurt, did it not? Then let the kissing-the-better ritual finish the job.\n5) Retell it at tea: you fell, it hurt, you had a cuddle, and now — better! The story with the ending is what makes stumbles survivable memory.',
 say:'"Hurt. This face has an ouch — see the tears? When you feel hurt, I come first, with cuddles. Then we say hurt together. Kiss it better, cuddle it better — and the hurt gets smaller and smaller."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child having a mild hurt feeling — one teary face with a small ouch. Ask your toddler to find the hurt face, then to give the card a gentle pat or a pretend kiss better.',
 ask:'"Where is the hurt face?" "What does the hurt face need?" "What comes first — cuddles or words?" "All better now?"',
 childSay:'Ouch arrives years before hurt does — the sound is the honest first word. Expect tears in real moments, a pointing finger at the sore bit, and, on the card, a gentle pat or a blown kiss.',
 real:'Real bonks run the routine for you: scoop, comfort, wait, name, retell. The card comes out after, when the knee is forgotten — that is the moment the feeling becomes a word with a story attached.',
 play:'Teddy hospital: a soft toy with a sore paw, a cuddle, a pretend kiss, a bandage made of a sock. Your toddler as the comforter learns the routine from the giving side — which is how it becomes automatic.',
 check:'After the next small injury, listen once it settles: ouch, hurt, or a point at the card mid-cuddle all pass. The deeper mark: your toddler bringing you the sore finger calmly, already knowing the order of events.',
 tips:'In the moment, comfort outranks teaching, always — the card is for afterwards. Keep the retelling short and kind: one fall, one cuddle, one better. Toddlers re-read these little stories more often than you think.',
 safety:'Cuddle first, assess second: check the bump properly once the tears slow — a real injury is grown-up work, not a kissing game, and help gets called when it looks beyond a cuddle. The card never substitutes for checking.'},
'loved':{
 title:'Feeling Loved',one:'The feeling your toddler knows best of all, finally with a word — loved is the constant running under every other feeling.',
 seoTitle:'Loved Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: say loved at cuddle time, link the word to the warmth, and make it a constant instead of a prize.',
 intro:'This lesson gives the deepest feeling its name: loved. The card pairs the word with the warmth, and the week makes sure the word arrives on ordinary days, not just special ones.',
 what:'Loved is the warm, safe, held feeling — cuddles, tucked blankets, being found in a crowd. Your toddler has lived it since day one; the card simply hands the feeling its word.',
 mean:'Love named becomes love owned: a toddler who hears loved attached to cuddles and ordinary Tuesdays learns the feeling is permanent, not earned. That security is the platform every other feeling stands on.',
 know:'Expect a hug, a grin, or loved said as a happy mumble into your shoulder. There is nothing to test here — the lesson is the saying, and the saying is the lesson.',
 why:'The word costs nothing and pays forever. Said at bedtime, after a storm, in the middle of nothing at all, it builds the constant your toddler will stand on for years.',
 dev:'Attachment language, security, and emotional vocabulary for the feeling under all feelings — the word that makes the rest of the deck safe to explore.',
 vocab:'loved, love, hug, cuddle, kiss, warm',
 teach:'1) Show the card at cuddle time with your toddler already in your arms: loved — this face is feeling loved. Let the word land inside the hug.\n2) Connect word and warmth: this face shows loved — it is exactly how I feel about you. Say it slowly, once, and let the cuddle finish.\n3) Ask where the loved face is; a point from inside the hug is the perfect answer.\n4) Say loved at ordinary moments all week: after breakfast, after a storm, halfway through shoes. The word should feel like furniture, not a fireworks display.\n5) Let your toddler give it back: a hug offered to you or to teddy is loved spoken in their own language — receive it like the news it is.',
 say:'"Loved. This face is feeling loved — warm and safe and snug. That is my heart about you! I love you at cuddle time, I love you after storms, I love you all day long. Come here — cuddle time."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child feeling loved — one warm, cosy face. Ask your toddler to find the loved face, then to give someone nearby a hug to match it.',
 ask:'"Where is the loved face?" "How does a cuddle feel?" "Who loves you?" "Who do you love?"',
 childSay:'A hug that arrives as the answer, a loved said with milk still on the top lip, or a kiss blown at the card — the feeling barely needs the word. Expect to be hugged mid-sentence; accept it as fluency.',
 real:'Loved moments are everywhere already: the towel at bath time, the reunion at the gate, the last story. Attach the word to two a day and the feeling gets its name on the regular.',
 play:'Love-round game: at bedtime, name three people who love your toddler — one, two, three, each with a squeeze. The roll call turns love from a feeling into a list your toddler can hold.',
 check:'There is no test for being loved — the pass mark is the cuddle itself. Watch instead for the word arriving in your toddler’s play: a teddy told it is loved means the vocabulary is moving in for good.',
 tips:'Say it on flat days as much as golden ones — loved needs to feel like a steady constant, never a reward for behaviour. The word after a meltdown teaches the deepest version: love that does not depend on the weather.',
 safety:'No safety angle here — this is the safest word in the deck. The honest note: keep it unconditional. Loved said at tantrums and triumphs alike is what makes the word load-bearing.'},
'friendly':{
 title:'Feeling Friendly',one:'A wave hello with the whole arm — friendly is the feeling that walks up to the world and says hi first.',
 seoTitle:'Friendly Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: practise the friendly wave, say hello to toys first, and let real greetings happen at the child’s own pace.',
 intro:'This lesson names the outward feeling: friendly, the hello-feeling that waves its whole arm. The card gives it a face mid-wave, and the games give it somewhere safe to practise.',
 what:'Friendly is the open, welcoming feeling — a wave, a smile, a hello offered to dogs, babies and postwomen alike. The card shows it in motion: one illustrated child, mid-wave.',
 mean:'Friendliness is a feeling and a skill at once: the warmth arrives on its own, and the greeting — wave, hello, smile — is the part that improves with practice. Toys make a patient practice crowd.',
 know:'Expect a big arm wave, a hello or hi delivered to the card, or a wave aimed at the window. Greetings to strangers remain optional and adult-supervised — the practice happens at home first.',
 why:'Greetings structure a toddler’s whole social day: arriving, leaving, meeting, waving bye. A named friendly feeling with a rehearsed wave turns each one into a small victory lap.',
 dev:'Social greeting skills, feeling vocabulary for the outgoing moods, and confidence in first moves — the emotional machinery of making friends.',
 vocab:'friendly, hello, wave, hi, bye, smile',
 teach:'1) Show the card and wave at it: friendly — this face is saying hello! Wave back at the card from your toddler’s hand if they need a start.\n2) Practise the wave in the mirror: big wave, small wave, wave with both arms. Hello can be whispered, shouted, or just waved.\n3) Run greetings on the toys: wave hello to teddy, wave bye-bye to the duck, say hi to the bus rumbling past. Six greets a day keeps the wave warm.\n4) At real arrivals, offer the choice: hello, wave, or just watch? Any of the three counts as friendly — the feeling is the point, the words are optional extras.\n5) Close the loop on goodbyes too: friendly has a second act — wave bye-bye, blow a kiss. Endings done warmly are half of friendliness.',
 say:'"Friendly! This face is waving hello — look at that big wave! Can you wave? Hello, hello! We wave hello, we wave bye-bye. Being friendly is saying hi in your own way — a wave is plenty."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child giving a friendly wave — one open face with a hand in the air. Ask your toddler to find the friendly face, then to wave back at it just as big.',
 ask:'"Where is the friendly face?" "Can you wave like that?" "Who shall we say hello to?" "Can you wave bye-bye?"',
 childSay:'A full-arm wave with optional sound effects — ha! or hi! — is the complete answer. Some toddlers wave at everything for a day, including the fridge; the enthusiasm is the lesson taking hold.',
 real:'Greets happen all day at toddler height: the neighbour, the bus driver, the dog on the other side of the fence. Each wave is a repetition, and each one is free.',
 play:'Wave parade: march through the house greeting everything — hello chair, hello window, bye-bye bath. The parade makes greetings a game and the game makes the real ones easy.',
 check:'At the next real arrival, watch for the wave — instant, delayed, or from behind your leg, all pass. A hello aimed at a teddy before the real person counts too; the practice crowd gets credit.',
 tips:'Let the child choose the volume and the timing of every hello — the feeling is theirs to spend. Your job is the warm example: wave first yourself, and let friendliness be caught rather than taught.',
 safety:'Real greetings happen on your terms: grown-ups decide who gets close, hands get held near roads, and waving from your hip is always a valid distance. Friendly and careful are the same lesson.'},
'kind':{
 title:'Feeling Kind',one:'The feeling behind the gentle pat and the offered crumb — kind happens dozens of times a day and rarely gets named. This card changes that.',
 seoTitle:'Kind Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: catch kindness in the act, name it out loud, and practise gentle hands on toys, pets and people.',
 intro:'This lesson catches the quiet feeling with the biggest reputation: kind. The card shows a child being kind and helpful, and the week is full of small versions waiting to be named.',
 what:'Kind is the helping, gentle feeling — the soft pat, the shared bite, the blanket carried to a cold teddy. The card shows it in action: a child being kind and helpful.',
 mean:'Kindness named gets repeated: a toddler who hears that was kind! while the gentle hands are still gentle learns exactly what the word points at. Named kindness is how a habit begins.',
 know:'Catch it small and in the moment: the pat, the crumb, the delivery of a shoe. Expect a grin, a kind repeated, or more gentleness on the spot — the behaviour blooms under attention.',
 why:'Kindness happens hourly at two and almost never gets a headline. Naming it turns scattered gentle moments into a self-image: I am someone who is kind.',
 dev:'Prosocial behaviour, gentle-hands habits, and feeling vocabulary for the caring moods — the emotional start of ethics, built on teddy care.',
 vocab:'kind, gentle, help, share, soft, care',
 teach:'1) Show the card and name the action inside the feeling: kind — this child is being kind and helpful. Look how gentle.\n2) Practise gentle hands right there: stroke your own arm, stroke your toddler’s arm, then let them stroke yours. Slow is the whole secret.\n3) Run it on the toys: teddy gets a blanket, dolly gets a pat, the rabbit gets a gentle touch. Say kind each time the hands stay soft.\n4) Catch the real thing and name it instantly: you gave Teddy your blanket — how kind! The naming must land while the moment is still warm.\n5) Let kindness be contagious: when your toddler is kind to you, say so, and accept it. The feeling needs to be received as much as practised.',
 say:'"Kind! This child is being kind — gentle hands, helping. Can you do gentle hands? Stroke, stroke — soft. You gave Teddy a blanket — that was kind! Kind means helping and being gentle. You know how to do that."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child being kind and helpful — one caring face, one helping gesture. Ask your toddler to find the kind child, then to show you their own gentle hands.',
 ask:'"Where is the kind child?" "Can you do gentle hands?" "How did you help today?" "Was that kind or rough?"',
 childSay:'A pat that slows down mid-air when the word lands, a kind echoed proudly, or a teddy wrapped without prompting — all are the word taking root. Rough moments are information too: gentle hands, said once, and try again.',
 real:'Kindness grows at home on real targets: the baby, the cat, the visitor, you. One named kind moment a day is the whole curriculum — and it compounds.',
 play:'Gentle-zoo: gather the soft toys and visit each one with careful strokes — kind hands for the lion, kind hands for the bear. The zoo runs on the same skill the family cat is hoping for.',
 check:'Watch for gentleness arriving without prompting: a soft pat, a shared bite, a helped-up friend. Name each one. A toddler who says kind while doing it has closed the loop entirely.',
 tips:'Notice kindness rather than demanding it — the feeling grows on being caught, not commanded. Keep the bar low and the catches frequent: a single gentle pat is headline material at two.',
 safety:'Gentle hands have real destinations: pets get stroked on the back with permission, babies get supervised hellos, and grown-ups stay within reach of every new meeting. Kindness and safety practice the same hands.'},
'brave':{
 title:'Feeling Brave',one:'Scared and doing it anyway — brave is not the absence of wobbles but the wobbles plus one more step, which your toddler takes daily.',
 seoTitle:'Brave Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Emotions and Feelings (Class 13)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: name the brave feeling after hard tries, retell the wobble-and-do-it story, and keep real danger a grown-up job.',
 intro:'This lesson names the feeling your toddler produces daily without applause: brave. The card shows a child being brave by trying, and the lesson retells those moments as true stories with heroes.',
 what:'Brave is the wobbly-and-anyway feeling — the big slide attempted, the loud dryer endured, the new food tasted. The card shows it honestly: a child being brave by trying, not by winning.',
 mean:'Brave is scared plus one step: the two feelings share a body, and the story that joins them — it was big, the wobble was real, and then you did it — is what teaches a toddler their own courage.',
 know:'Keep the word for genuine effort by your toddler’s own measure — their hard, not yours. Expect a grin, a brave repeated, or the deed done again immediately. All pass; the immediate repeat most of all.',
 why:'Hard things arrive hourly at two, and each one is a story waiting for its ending. A named brave turns scattered trials into a self-image with a spine.',
 dev:'Confidence, effort vocabulary, and the fear-to-action arc — emotional storytelling that makes courage a habit instead of an accident.',
 vocab:'brave, try, big, step, scared, did',
 teach:'1) Show the card and tell its story: brave — this child is trying something big, even though it feels scary. Brave means scared, and doing it anyway.\n2) Make the face together: determined chin, deep breath, fists ready. The posture is half the feeling — borrow it freely.\n3) Catch it live at the next wobble-and-try: you felt scared, and you went down the slide anyway — brave! Say it while the landing is still bouncy.\n4) Retell the day’s brave moment at dinner as a three-beat story: the big thing, the scared feeling, the doing it anyway. Your toddler takes the hero role, truthfully.\n5) Keep real danger where it belongs: brave is for the child’s own hard things — grown-ups own roads, water and heights. Say the boundary once so the word stays safe to use.',
 say:'"Brave! This child is trying the big thing — see the brave face? Brave is feeling scared, and doing it anyway. You know what? You do that all the time! You are brave. Let us tell the story of when you did it."',
 notice:'The card shows an illustrated child showing bravery by trying — one determined face mid-attempt. Ask your toddler to find the brave child, then to make their own brave face and take one big pretend step.',
 ask:'"Where is the brave child?" "Can you make a brave face?" "What big thing did you try today?" "What happened next?"',
 childSay:'A determined march toward the thing, a brave whispered at the top of the slide, or the story retold with the hero’s part pointed at themselves — all are the word moving in. Wobbles still happen; brave includes them.',
 real:'Brave moments line up daily: the hand-dryer, the big step, the new babysitter. Catch one, tell it as a story, and the word starts doing quiet work in the background of every hard thing.',
 play:'Story-theatre: retell today’s brave moment with toys playing the parts — the slide, the wobble, the try. Your toddler directs, corrects and casts themselves as the hero, which is accurate casting.',
 check:'At the next hard thing, listen afterwards: brave, did it, or a point at the card during the retelling all pass. Then watch for the second attempt happening sooner — courage compounding is the real report card.',
 tips:'Save the word for genuine effort by your toddler’s own measure — brave spent on easy things goes bankrupt fast. Tell the wobble part honestly: the scared bit is what makes the brave bit mean something.',
 safety:'Brave is for wobbles, not hazards: roads, water, fires and high places stay grown-up decisions, always and without negotiation. Say it plainly — you are brave, and I keep you safe — so both truths can hold hands.'}
};
